    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec adds a `riskFlag` on the owner representation, true when **any** of three conditions holds. Two were already encoded elsewhere in the create pipeline, so I reused those semantics rather than reinventing them.\n\n**Three changes, three files:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `riskFlag` boolean property to the `Owner` schema. `OwnerDto` is generated from this spec, so the field and its `setRiskFlag`/`getRiskFlag` accessors come for free \u2014 no hand-edited DTO class (avoids touching a large generated class).\n\n2. **`RiskFlag.java`** (new, small cohesive helper, mirrors the existing `PossibleDuplicate` / `CapacityWarning` helpers) \u2014 `isActive(owner, repo)` ORs the three conditions:\n   - **possible duplicate** \u2192 reuses `PossibleDuplicate.of(...) != null`\n   - **disposable-adjacent email** \u2192 domain carrying a label of a blocked disposable provider (e.g. a different TLD or subdomain of `mailinator`/`tempmail`/`guerrillamail`); exact blocks are already rejected at create time, so \"adjacent\" is the meaningful residual check\n   - **city over soft capacity** \u2192 `count >= 40` (the soft threshold below the hard cap of 50)\n\n3. **`RespondWithOwner.java`** (the `GET /api/owners/{id}` responder the test reads back through) \u2014 one line: `dto.setRiskFlag(RiskFlag.isActive(owner, ownerRepository));`\n\n**Structural-impact notes:**\n- I put the city-count directly in `RiskFlag` rather than reusing `CapacityWarning.isActive`, because that helper is bounded to `40\u201349` and would wrongly report *false* for a city sitting exactly at the hard cap of 50 \u2014 \"over soft capacity\" is `count >= 40` with no upper bound.\n- The only edited method is `RespondWithOwner.service` (CC 1, no sibling methods \u2192 near-zero WMC), plus a new self-contained helper class. No existing method grew in complexity and no large class was modified.",
